us writer director Jordan peels  much-anticipated follow-up to his horror  masterpiece get out is finally here and  it was worth the wait what starts as an  apparently simple home invasion Fright  Fest quickly evolves into something  bigger as Pele spins bigger and thicker  ideas into a movie with bigger and  bigger implications the result is a  conceptually weighty horror film that  keeps you guessing and thinking right up  to the very last minute and that means  we've got plenty to talk about with that  in mind here are the best and worst  things about us oh and it should go  without saying but there are spoilers  ahead for the entire movie you've been  warned horror cinema has a huge capacity  for pushing actors to their emotional  and physical limits and the genres long  tradition of placing vulnerable women at  the center of stories means we've gotten  truly legendary performances out of some  major talents from Jamie Lee Curtis to  Sigourney Weaver to Toni Collette  Adelaide played by Oscar winner Lupita  Nyong'o is the center of us and she  rises to the occasion by adding another  legendary performance to horror film  history a performance made all the more  powerful by her dual roles as Adelaide  she is vulnerable but strong scared but  determined loving with savage as red  Adelaide's tethered double she is both  an entirely different personality and  every bit Adelaide's mirror image  she jerks in twists when Adelaide is  graceful grins when Adelaide gasps in  terror and croaks for Adelaide speaks  it's a magnificent pair of performances  and while there are certainly plenty of  other things to recommend about us her  daring and brilliant performance is the  first thing many moviegoers will come  away talking about the Tyler family who  joined the Wilsons for a day at the  beach in the first act of the film are  presented as shallow bickering people  with no real sense of love between them  while that creates plenty of  opportunities for comedy it does mean  the blow is dulled a little bit when  their murders happen midway through the  movie yes that sequence is funny and  gets quite tense when the Wilsons show  up but you get the sense that the  Tyler's didn't really need to be as  broad as they are particularly when the  film already roots us in the emotional  reality of the Wilsons before he was an  acclaimed genius of horror Jordan Peele  was an acclaimed genius of comedy thanks  to his collaborate  with keegan-michael key which resulted  in the TV series key and Peele and the  film Keanu Peele never made some kind of  hard cut from one genre to the other  though as a horror fan he has an innate  understanding of the role comedy plays  in scary movies something he  demonstrated over and over again with  his debut horror feature get out how do  you find me I'm TS mother we handle us  is another masterclass in balancing  laughs and scares beginning with the  answer play of the Wilson family as they  travel together in the car and  continuing throughout the entire film  don't do drugs like his horror deals  comedy start small with little dad jokes  from Gabe and comforting family banter  it continues to build from there until  its intertwining with the scares it's  Gabe wrestles with his tethered double  on his new boat ultimately in the most  desperate situations in the film the  comedy provides a welcome sense of fun  as the Wilson family can't help  commenting on how bizarre their  situation is even as they're trying to  survive it all of that plus a welcomed  needle drop from NWA means that us is  often as hilarious as it is terrifying  us begins with a relatively tight  concept evil duplicates are invading and  trying to kill us  as the film expands and expands it keeps  expanding until it essentially has  nationwide implications and the themes  just keep expanding with it because of  that expansion us gets messy and not  just in terms of the body count the film  swells until it becomes not just about  our own dark sides but about how some  people prosper while others suffer in an  unfair system and how the powerful  manipulate regular people in unseen ways  and about the empty gestures of charity  and hope that are mostly about public  relations and about the links we'll go  to in order to take what we feel we  deserve and so on and so on these are  all valid themes but by the end the film  tries to throw all these things at us at  once and some of them land hard while  others simply fall by the wayside it's a  shotgun approach to the symbolism at the  heart of the movie and it doesn't work  as well as it should over  it could even be said that without get  out to compare it to we wouldn't notice  the difference but it's there and it's  noticeable still you got to get the  movie credit even if it's reject seeds  its grasp at least it tried to reach at  all with us Jordan Peele created some  legendary creepy nightmare is called the  tavern and they live up to all the  promise they had in the films trailers  stick with me stain the design of the  tethered is deceptively simple and even  though we don't learn why they look the  way they do red jumpsuits gloves sandals  and scissors until late in the film  their look works from the very beginning  when we see them standing in the Wilson  family's driveway silent and stoic as  the concept that the movies heart  continues expanding and its pathology  gets more complex the tethered  themselves remain deceptively simple  movie monsters their behavior can change  a bit and the motives can become more  clear but the deliberate almost robotic  way in which they pursue their goals  does not and that makes them terrifying  they're extremely effective movie  monsters thanks in part to peels less is  more approach and the actors inhabiting  them make them unforgettable slasher  villains as the film's final act leads  Adelaide down into a series of tunnels  we finally learn what the tethered  really are genetic copies of people  living on the surface one human and body  but vacant in spirit who were created to  serve his underground puppets for the  powerful to manipulate the citizenry it  eventually all went wrong and the  tethered were simply left in the tunnels  to fend for themselves until read  herself began to organize the uprising  we see in the film it's an intriguing  explanation for a lot of seemingly  unrelated things but the way it's  finally revealed to us with read  explaining it all via voiceover could  have been better we've seen this villain  do all manner of frightening  unpredictable and compelling things and  now in this moment she's reduced to  monologuing in the dark room because the  film has to dish out a whole bunch of  information in a very short time it's  perhaps the one in elegant thing the  film has to offer and it still manages  to be interesting even if it's clunky  peel is such a talented writer skilled  at wielding high concepts in new ways  mixing comedy and drama and pacing in a  way that makes it look easy that he  could shoot his films in a style that's  merely competent and still come away  with hits as long as everything's in  focus and the editing isn't confusing  the stew  we will still work and the characters  will still come through peel isn't  content to just keep things adequate  though and that shows through and the  visual flourishes present throughout us  every frame of this film looks amazing  it doesn't matter if you're a fan of  horror or not this is some of the finest  visual storytelling you're likely to see  in any movie in 2019 the eventual  explanation for who and what the  tethered really are and why they were  created as an intriguing one and helps  to set up that amazing final shot of  bloody hands across America but things  start to fall apart the more you think  about the logistics of it all why was  the entrance to the tethered underground  tunnels unlocked why didn't more wander  to the surface world more often why can  the tethered act with free will in some  scenes and become mirror images of their  counterparts the next there are probably  great explanations for these questions  but the film is too interested in moving  relentlessly forward to give them to us  and that might take a little air out of  the story's tires if you get stuck  thinking too much about it all on that  same subject it's confirmed in the  movies final moments that Adelaide is  actually the original tethered and read  was the original Adelaide who she  strangled and locked up in the tunnels  after that first carnival encounter all  those years ago the twist works because  it's the kind of thing that just burrows  into your brain as the credits are  rolling but it also feels like the last  brick that almost makes the tower  beneath it start to topple if there's  one impulse the movie maybe should have  resisted it might be this one unless we  get to explore the true implications of  this revelation and the inevitable  sequel us to a bus full of us now that  sounds scary check out one of our newest  videos right here plus even more looper  videos about your favorite horror movies  are coming soon subscribe to our YouTube  channel and hit the bell so you don't  miss a single one  [Music]  [Applause]  [Music]
 

